\section{One common marginal and conditional moments}
Write $q=1-p$, $v=pq$, and suppose the centered conditional residuals satisfy
\[
 U_0\in[-L_0,R_0]=[-O_0,a_0],\qquad
 U_1\in[-L_1,R_1]=[-a_1,O_1],\qquad
 \E U_i=0,
\]
with $w_i=\E U_i^2$ and $m_i=\E U_i^3$. Endpoints here may be acquired upper
caps containing the actual support. Let $s=qw_0+pw_1$ and $h^2=v+s$.
The actual-law identity supplied by the conditional-coordinate reduction is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:rho}
 \rho h^3=C(p,w_0,w_1)-q m_0+p m_1,
 \qquad C=v(1-2v)+3v(w_0+w_1).
\end{equation}
The same $m_0,m_1$ must be used in this identity and the count moment bound.
All conclusions below assume that identity and the actual-law resource
intervals have been licensed upstream.

\begin{lemma}[Cubic bounds]\label{lem:cubic}
If $\E U=0$, $\E U^2=w$, and $-L\le U\le R$ with $L,R>0$, then
\[
 \frac{w^2}{L}-Lw\ \le\ \E U^3\ \le\ Rw-\frac{w^2}{R}.
\]
If either endpoint is zero, $U=0$ almost surely and both moments vanish.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The nonnegative polynomial $(R-U)(U+w/R)^2$ has expectation
$Rw-w^2/R-\E U^3$. Apply the same argument to $-U$ for the lower bound.
A centered random variable supported on only one side of zero vanishes.
\end{proof}
For a variance interval $w\in[b,c]$, the valid enclosing lower cubic endpoint
is the minimum of $w^2/L-Lw$, attained at
$\max(b,\min(c,L^2/2))$. The upper endpoint is the maximum of
$Rw-w^2/R$, attained at $\max(b,\min(c,R^2/2))$.
Checking only the two interval endpoints is generally invalid.
Feasibility also gives $w\le LR$; clipping the variance interval to this bound
is valid. Increasing either support cap only weakens its corresponding
one-sided cubic bound. Thus the resulting rational rectangle
$[x_-,x_+]\times[y_-,y_+]$ contains the actual pair $(m_0,m_1)$.

\section{A rational strip retaining the actual rho}
Let $p\in[p_-,p_+]\subset(0,1/2]$, put
$p_*=(p_-+p_+)/2$ and $\epsilon=(p_+-p_-)/2$, and suppose
$\rho\in[r_-,r_+]$. Let $N_-\le\rho h^3\le N_+$ and
$C_-\le C\le C_+$ be outward bounds over the entire parameter cell. Define
\[
 M=\max\{|x_-+y_-|,|x_++y_+|\},\qquad
 A=-(1-p_*),\quad B=p_*.
\]
\begin{lemma}[Common-rho strip]
Every actual cubic pair belongs to the closed rational polytope
\[
 \mathcal P=([x_-,x_+]\times[y_-,y_+])\cap
 \{(x,y):N_--C_+-\epsilon M\le Ax+By
                         \le N_+-C_-+\epsilon M\}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:rho} gives exactly
\[
 A m_0+B m_1=\rho h^3-C-(p-p_*)(m_0+m_1).
\]
The rectangle implies $|m_0+m_1|\le M$, proving both strip inequalities.
\end{proof}
For example, with $v_-=p_-(1-p_-)$, $v_+=p_+(1-p_+)$ and
$s\in[s_-,s_+]\subset[0,\infty)$, valid choices are
\[
 N_-=r_-\,\underline{\sqrt{(v_-+s_-)^3}},\qquad
 N_+=r_+\,\overline{\sqrt{(v_++s_+)^3}}.
\]
The underlined and overlined quantities are rational outward root bounds.
Because $v(1-2v)$ increases for $0\le v\le1/4$ and all variances are
nonnegative, valid bounds for $C$ follow by using all lower or all upper
endpoints. Dependency loss in these bounds weakens the strip but does not
invalidate it. Any separately proved local lower or upper rho bound may
replace the corresponding branch endpoint by intersection.

\section{Quartic expectation and phase}
Condition on a fixed count $k$ among $n$ iid summands. The conditional
sum is distributed as a sum $W$ of $N_0=n-k$ independent copies of $U_0$
and $N_1=k$ independent copies of $U_1$. Its variance is
$V=N_0w_0+N_1w_1$, its third moment is $N_0m_0+N_1m_1$, and independence gives
\[
 \E W^4=3V^2-3(N_0w_0^2+N_1w_1^2)
              +N_0\E U_0^4+N_1\E U_1^4.
\]
The pointwise nonnegative polynomial $U^2(U+L)(R-U)$ gives
\[
 \E U^4\le LRw+(R-L)m.
\]
This remains valid for any acquired larger support caps, while preserving the
\emph{same} actual cubic moment $m$. Thus
\begin{align*}
 \E W^4\le{}&3V^2-3(N_0w_0^2+N_1w_1^2)\\
 &+N_0\{O_0a_0w_0+(a_0-O_0)m_0\}
  +N_1\{a_1O_1w_1+(O_1-a_1)m_1\}.
\end{align*}
The cross term has the alternative expression
\[
 3N_0(N_0-1)w_0^2+3N_1(N_1-1)w_1^2+6N_0N_1w_0w_1,
\]
which increases in each nonnegative variance. This offers another safe
interval enclosure; it need not improve every existing coupled variance bound.

For any fixed phase $t$, the resulting upper bound on
$\E(W-t)^4$ is affine in $(m_0,m_1)$, with coefficients
\[
 N_0(a_0-O_0-4t),\qquad N_1(O_1-a_1-4t).
\]
For a quartic polynomial with nonnegative leading coefficient, this substitution
gives an affine upper bound in the same way. A negative leading coefficient
instead gives a lower bound; a subsequent score calculation must preserve
that direction. Maximizing an affine upper bound over $\mathcal P$ reduces
to its vertices. For a whole phase interval,
each vertex gives a polynomial in $t$ whose coefficients have rational
outward enclosures. Applying the existing interval Bernstein upper bound to
\emph{every} vertex polynomial gives a valid uniform upper bound. This does
not differentiate an actual CDF, exchange a minimizer with a supremum, or
require a maximizing vertex to be realizable by an iid marginal.

\section{Degeneracies, events, and implementation review}
\begin{itemize}
\item A closed rectangle intersected with a strip has vertices among feasible
rectangle corners and intersections of a rectangle edge with either strip
boundary. Exact rational edge interpolation covers collapsed rectangles,
zero-width strips, duplicate vertices, and boundary coincidences. A constant
edge is covered by its endpoints. An exactly empty polytope excludes the
entire parameter cell as a necessary-condition contradiction.
\item Zero variance forces the corresponding cubic moment to zero. A zero
support endpoint also forces zero actual variance; a variance interval with
positive lower endpoint is then infeasible. Zero endpoint divisions must be
handled before evaluating the formulas. Counts $k=0,n$ are allowed.
\item The stated monotonic bounds for $v$ use $0<p_-\le p_+\le1/2$.
The endpoint $p_+=1/2$ is included. A reflected probability half-domain must
transform the residuals and cubic signs consistently; it is not licensed by
silently reusing an asymmetric formula.
\item All moment inequalities are non-strict and may be attained. They do
not resolve the distinction between $\Pr(W<t)$ and $\Pr(W\le t)$ at atoms.
The outgoing event bound must still be proved pointwise at the event plane
with the intended strict or closed convention. Final strict score exclusion
requires a separate strict rational margin; equality in this relaxation
does not provide one.
\item The strip must be valid for the whole phase/parameter cell. Shared
moments cannot be recomputed independently for the normalization and quartic
cost. Allowing infeasible vertices is safe only in the direction of a larger
upper bound, never as evidence that an actual iid extremizer exists.
\end{itemize}
The reviewed prototype \texttt{joint\_cubic.py}, archived with its caller in
the local review snapshot, has the correct variance stationary-point clamps,
strip sign and phase-free
error, exact degenerate vertex enumeration, and affine cubic coefficients in
the inherited quartic score polynomial. Its caller clips
$w_0\le O_0a_0$, $w_1\le a_1O_1$ and has nonnegative variance intervals.
The caller can further intersect the branch rho upper bound with its already
proved local \texttt{rho\_law\_upper}. Taking the minimum with the old valid
envelope is safe. Code review alone is not an accepted continuous-domain
certificate; malformed-object tests and fresh arithmetic acceptance remain
separate obligations.

\section{A second half-plane from the actual defect}
Let $\sigma=\sqrt{10}$, let $D\le D_*$ be the acquired actual defect bound,
and retain actual inward endpoints $\ell_i\le a_i\le u_i$ satisfying
$a_0+a_1\le d<1/4$. The before-cubic inequality in the retained conditional
defect proof is
\begin{align*}
 D h^3\ge{}&B+\alpha m_0+\beta m_1,\\
 B={}&b_0(p)+6v f(a_0,a_1)
  +q w_0\{3(\sigma+3)p-6a_0\}
  +p w_1\{3(\sigma+1)q-6a_1\},\\
 f(a_0,a_1)={}&a_0+a_1-a_0^2-a_1^2-a_0a_1,\\
 \alpha={}&-(\sigma+1)(1-p),\qquad \beta=(\sigma-1)p,\\
 b_0(p)={}&v\{(\sigma+1)p^2+(\sigma-1)q^2-6v\}.
\end{align*}
This is a lower inequality for $D$, not an identity. Its retained proof uses
the actual support-gap functional $J$, with central and outer gaps; $J$ must
not be replaced by a pairwise absolute moment or by a coarse bound as an
equality. Expanding that proof's conditional $T_\pm,R_\pm$ expressions gives
exactly the displayed before-cubic expression. Substituting Lemma~1 into it
recovers the earlier sharp conditional defect bound.

Let $\sigma_-\le\sigma\le\sigma_+$, with $\sigma_->1$, be rational root
bounds. On the actual simplex, $\partial_0f=1-2a_0-a_1\ge1-2d>0$, and
similarly for $\partial_1f$. Thus $f(a_0,a_1)\ge f(\ell_0,\ell_1)$;
the path from the lower corner to an actual point stays inside that simplex.
Assume the rational coefficients
\[
 c_0=3(\sigma_-+3)p_--6u_0\ge0,\qquad
 c_1=3(\sigma_-+1)(1-p_+)-6u_1\ge0.
\]
A valid base lower bound is
\[
 B_-=b_-+6v_-f(\ell_0,\ell_1)
       +(1-p_+)w_{0,-}c_0+p_-w_{1,-}c_1.
\]
Here $b_-$ is an outward lower bound for $b_0$ after substituting $\sigma_-$.
The substitution is valid because $\partial b_0/\partial\sigma
=v(p^2+q^2)\ge0$. It is also valid to clamp $b_-$ to zero: the exact
$\sigma=\sqrt{10}$ identity
\[
 b_0(p)=v(6+2\sigma)\left(p-\frac{4-\sigma}{2}\right)^2
\]
proves nonnegativity independently of the rational enclosure.

Set $\alpha_*=-(\sigma_-+1)(1-p_*)$ and
$\beta_*=(\sigma_--1)p_*$. With $\Delta\sigma=\sigma_+-\sigma_-$,
\begin{align*}
 |\alpha-\alpha_*|&\le\Delta\sigma(1-p_-)+(\sigma_-+1)\epsilon=:e_0,\\
 |\beta-\beta_*|&\le\Delta\sigma p_++(\sigma_--1)\epsilon=:e_1.
\end{align*}
For example,
$\alpha-\alpha_*=-(\sigma-\sigma_-)(1-p)
 +(\sigma_-+1)(p-p_*)$, which proves the first bound; the second is analogous.
Let $M_0=\max(|x_-|,|x_+|)$ and $M_1=\max(|y_-|,|y_+|)$.
If $D_*\ge0$ and $h^3\le H_+$, every actual cubic pair must satisfy
\[
 \boxed{\alpha_*m_0+\beta_*m_1
 \le D_*H_+-B_-+e_0M_0+e_1M_1.}
\]
Indeed, first use the lower defect inequality, then the upper $D_*$,
then $D_*\ge0$ to replace $h^3$ by $H_+$, and finally the coefficient-error
bounds. There is no valid reverse inequality from a lower bound on $D$.

Intersect this half-plane with $\mathcal P$. Its vertices can be enumerated
by exact intersections of every pair of nonparallel boundary lines, retaining
only points satisfying all seven closed inequalities. The four explicit
rectangle faces ensure boundedness; every nonempty resulting polygon, segment,
or point has vertices generated this way. A generic list of half-planes
without these bounding faces would require a separate boundedness argument.
The affine phase argument above applies to this smaller polytope unchanged.

